WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:23.120
I want you to imagine two couples in the middle of 1979 on the exact same day at the exact

00:23.120 --> 00:26.560
same moment each conceiving a baby.

00:26.560 --> 00:28.440
So two couples each conceiving one baby.

00:28.440 --> 00:31.440
I don't want you to spend too much time imagining the conception because if you spend all that

00:31.440 --> 00:34.240
time imagining that conception you're not going to listen to me.

00:34.240 --> 00:40.920
So just imagine that for a moment and in this scenario I want to imagine that in one case

00:40.920 --> 00:45.540
the sperm is carrying a Y chromosome meeting that X chromosome of the egg and in the other

00:45.540 --> 00:50.760
case the sperm is carrying an X chromosome meeting the X chromosome of the egg.

00:50.760 --> 00:53.360
Both are viable, both take off.

00:53.360 --> 00:55.440
We'll come back to these people later.

00:55.440 --> 00:59.520
So I wear two hats in most of what I do.

00:59.520 --> 01:02.500
As the one hat I do history of anatomy.

01:02.500 --> 01:07.920
I'm an historian by training and what I study in that case is the way that people have dealt

01:07.920 --> 01:13.400
with anatomy, meaning human bodies, animal bodies, how they dealt with bodily fluids,

01:13.400 --> 01:17.280
concepts of bodies, how they thought about bodies.

01:17.280 --> 01:23.160
The other hat that I've worn in my work is as an activist, as a patient advocate or I

01:23.160 --> 01:27.840
sometimes say as an inpatient advocate for people who are patients of doctors.

01:27.840 --> 01:32.280
In that case what I've worked with is people who have body types that challenge social

01:32.280 --> 01:33.500
norms.

01:33.500 --> 01:38.060
So some of what I've worked on for example is people who are conjoined twins, two people

01:38.060 --> 01:39.840
within one body.

01:39.840 --> 01:43.280
Some of what I've worked on is people who have dwarfism, so people who are much shorter

01:43.280 --> 01:44.960
than typical.

01:44.960 --> 01:49.600
And a lot of what I've worked on is people who have atypical sex, so people who don't

01:49.600 --> 01:53.120
have the standard male or the standard female body types.

01:53.120 --> 01:57.000
And as a general term we can use the term intersex for this.

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
Intersex comes in a lot of different forms.

01:59.000 --> 02:03.360
I'll just give you a few examples of the types of ways you can have sex that isn't standard

02:03.360 --> 02:04.980
for male or female.

02:04.980 --> 02:11.160
So in one instance you can have somebody who has an XY chromosomal basis and that SRY gene

02:11.160 --> 02:16.560
on the Y chromosome tells the protogonads which we all have in the fetal life to become

02:16.560 --> 02:17.560
testes.

02:17.560 --> 02:20.400
In the fetal life those testes are pumping out testosterone.

02:20.400 --> 02:26.000
But because this individual lacks receptors to hear that testosterone the body doesn't

02:26.000 --> 02:31.280
react to the testosterone and this is a syndrome called androgen insensitivity syndrome.

02:31.280 --> 02:34.160
So lots of levels of testosterone but no reaction to it.

02:34.160 --> 02:38.080
As a consequence the body develops more along the female typical path.

02:38.080 --> 02:40.380
When the child is born she looks like a girl.

02:40.380 --> 02:41.760
She is a girl.

02:41.760 --> 02:46.080
She is raised as a girl and it's often not until she hits puberty and she's growing and

02:46.080 --> 02:50.560
developing breasts but she's not getting her period that somebody figures out something's

02:50.560 --> 02:54.720
up here and they do some tests and figure out that instead of having ovaries inside

02:54.720 --> 02:58.600
in a uterus she actually has testes inside and she has a Y chromosome.

02:58.600 --> 03:02.160
Now what's important to understand is you may think of this person as really being male

03:02.160 --> 03:04.600
but they're really not.

03:04.600 --> 03:08.400
Females like males have in our bodies something called the adrenal glands.

03:08.400 --> 03:12.680
They're in the back of our body and the adrenal glands make androgens which are a masculinizing

03:12.680 --> 03:13.840
hormone.

03:13.880 --> 03:17.280
Most females like me, I believe myself to be a typical female, I don't actually know

03:17.280 --> 03:20.480
my chromosomal makeup but I think I'm probably typical.

03:20.480 --> 03:23.020
Most females like me are actually androgen sensitive.

03:23.020 --> 03:26.000
We're making androgens and we're responding to androgens.

03:26.000 --> 03:31.200
The consequence is that somebody like me has actually had a brain exposed to more androgens

03:31.200 --> 03:34.700
than the woman born with testes who has androgen insensitivity syndrome.

03:34.700 --> 03:36.060
So sex is really complicated.

03:36.060 --> 03:39.320
It's not just that intersex people are in the middle of all the sex spectrum.

03:39.320 --> 03:41.740
In some ways they can be all over the place.

03:41.740 --> 03:46.700
For example, a few years ago I got a call from a man who was 19 years old who was born

03:46.700 --> 03:52.320
a boy, raised a boy, had a girlfriend, had sex with his girlfriend, had a life as a guy

03:52.320 --> 03:56.100
and had just found out that he had ovaries and a uterus inside.

03:56.100 --> 04:00.500
What he had was an extreme form of a condition called congenital adrenal hyperplasia.

04:00.500 --> 04:06.340
He had XX chromosomes and in the womb his adrenal glands were in such high gear that

04:06.340 --> 04:11.040
it created essentially a masculine hormonal environment and as a consequence his genitals

04:11.040 --> 04:14.880
were masculinized, his brain was subject to the more typical masculine component of

04:14.880 --> 04:17.600
hormones and he was born looking like a boy.

04:17.600 --> 04:22.160
Nobody suspected anything and it was only when he had reached the age of 19 that he

04:22.160 --> 04:25.920
began to have enough medical problems actually from menstruating internally that doctors

04:25.920 --> 04:28.840
figured out that in fact he was female internally.

04:28.840 --> 04:33.160
Okay, so just one more quick example of a way you can have intersex.

04:33.160 --> 04:37.280
Some people who have XX chromosomes develop what are called ovotustes which is when you

04:37.280 --> 04:40.640
have ovarian tissue with testicular tissue wrapped around it.

04:40.640 --> 04:42.800
We're not exactly sure why that happens.

04:42.800 --> 04:45.680
So sex can come in lots of different varieties.

04:45.680 --> 04:52.720
The reason that children with these kinds of bodies whether it's dwarfism or it's conjoined

04:52.720 --> 04:58.120
twinning or it's an intersex type are often normalized by surgeons is not because it actually

04:58.120 --> 05:01.200
leaves them better off in terms of physical health.

05:01.200 --> 05:04.140
In many cases people are actually perfectly healthy.

05:04.140 --> 05:08.460
The reason they're often subject to various kinds of surgeries is because they threaten

05:08.460 --> 05:10.120
our social categories.

05:10.120 --> 05:14.600
Our system has been based typically on the idea that a particular kind of anatomy comes

05:14.600 --> 05:16.280
with a particular identity.

05:16.280 --> 05:20.040
So we have the concept that what it means to be a woman is to have a female identity.

05:20.040 --> 05:25.400
What it means to be a black person is allegedly to have an African anatomy in terms of your

05:25.400 --> 05:26.680
history.

05:26.680 --> 05:32.680
And so we have this terribly simplistic idea and when we're faced with a body that actually

05:32.680 --> 05:37.880
presents us something quite different it startles us in terms of those categorizations.

05:37.880 --> 05:41.920
So we have a lot of very romantic ideas in our culture about individualism.

05:41.920 --> 05:44.960
And our nation is really founded on a very romantic concept of individualism.

05:44.960 --> 05:48.920
Well you can imagine how startling then it is when you have children who are born who

05:48.920 --> 05:52.320
are two people inside of one body.

05:52.320 --> 05:56.640
Where I ran into the most kind of heat from this most recently was last year when the

05:56.640 --> 06:00.900
South African runner Caster Semenya had her sex called into question at the International

06:00.900 --> 06:02.160
Games in Berlin.

06:02.160 --> 06:06.400
I had a lot of journalists calling me asking me which is the test they're going to run

06:06.400 --> 06:10.560
that will tell us whether or not Caster Semenya is male or female.

06:10.560 --> 06:13.960
And I had to explain to the journalists there isn't such a test.

06:13.960 --> 06:20.160
In fact we now know that sex is complicated enough that we have to admit nature doesn't

06:20.160 --> 06:24.960
draw the line for us between male and female or between male and intersex and female and

06:24.960 --> 06:26.140
intersex.

06:26.140 --> 06:28.760
We actually draw that line on nature.

06:28.760 --> 06:33.960
So what we have is a sort of situation where the farther our science goes the more we have

06:33.960 --> 06:39.120
to admit to ourselves that these categories that we thought of as stable anatomical categories

06:39.120 --> 06:44.960
that mapped very simply to stable identity categories are a lot more fuzzy than we thought.

06:44.960 --> 06:46.920
And it's not just in terms of sex.

06:46.920 --> 06:51.680
It's also in terms of race which turns out to be vastly more complicated than our terminology

06:51.680 --> 06:52.680
has allowed.

06:52.680 --> 06:56.160
As we look we get into all sorts of sort of uncomfortable areas.

06:56.160 --> 07:02.480
We look for example about the fact that we share at least 95% of our DNA with chimpanzees.

07:02.480 --> 07:07.640
What are we to make of the fact that we differ from them only really by a few nucleotides?

07:07.640 --> 07:11.400
And as we get farther and farther with our science we get more and more into a discomforted

07:11.400 --> 07:16.080
zone where we have to acknowledge that the simplistic categories we've had are probably

07:16.080 --> 07:18.240
overly simplistic.

07:18.240 --> 07:21.800
So we're seeing this in all sorts of places in human life.

07:21.800 --> 07:25.040
One of the places we're seeing it for example in our culture today in the United States

07:25.040 --> 07:28.840
today is battles over the beginning of life and the end of life.

07:28.840 --> 07:33.480
We have difficult conversations about at what point we decide a body becomes a human

07:33.480 --> 07:36.660
such that it has a different right than a fetal life.

07:36.660 --> 07:41.200
We have very difficult conversations nowadays probably not out in the open as much as within

07:41.200 --> 07:44.240
medicine about the question of when somebody's dead.

07:44.240 --> 07:47.680
In the past our ancestors never had to struggle so much with this question of when somebody

07:47.680 --> 07:48.680
was dead.

07:48.680 --> 07:51.840
At most they'd stick a feather on somebody's nose and if it twitched they didn't bury them

07:51.840 --> 07:52.840
yet.

07:52.840 --> 07:54.960
If it stopped twitching you buried them.

07:54.960 --> 07:59.520
But today we have a situation where we want to take vital organs out of beings and give

07:59.520 --> 08:00.520
them to other beings.

08:00.520 --> 08:04.680
And as a consequence we're stuck with having to struggle with this really difficult question

08:04.680 --> 08:06.720
about who's dead.

08:06.720 --> 08:10.120
And this leads us to a really difficult situation where we don't have such simple categories

08:10.120 --> 08:11.680
as we've had before.

08:11.680 --> 08:15.920
Now you might think that all this breaking down of categories would make somebody like

08:15.920 --> 08:17.000
me really happy.

08:17.000 --> 08:18.320
I'm a political progressive.

08:18.320 --> 08:23.040
I defend people with unusual bodies but I have to admit to you that it makes me nervous.

08:23.040 --> 08:27.560
Knowing that these categories are really much more unstable than we thought makes me tense.

08:27.560 --> 08:30.760
And it makes me tense from the point of view of thinking about democracy.

08:30.760 --> 08:34.240
So in order to tell you about that tension I have to first admit to you that I'm a huge

08:34.240 --> 08:36.080
fan of the founding fathers.

08:36.080 --> 08:37.520
I know they were racist.

08:37.520 --> 08:38.840
I know they were sexist.

08:38.840 --> 08:39.840
But they were great.

08:39.840 --> 08:46.320
I mean they were so brave and so bold and so radical in what they did that I find myself

08:46.320 --> 08:50.480
watching that cheesy musical 1776 every few years and it's not because of the music which

08:50.480 --> 08:52.000
is totally forgettable.

08:52.000 --> 08:55.920
It's because of what happened in 1776 with the founding fathers.

08:55.920 --> 09:00.400
The founding fathers were from my point of view the original anatomical activists.

09:00.400 --> 09:02.080
And this is why.

09:02.080 --> 09:06.960
What they rejected was an anatomical concept and replaced it with another one that was

09:06.960 --> 09:10.220
radical and beautiful and held us for 200 years.

09:10.220 --> 09:15.380
So as you will recall what our founding fathers were rejecting was a concept of monarchy.

09:15.380 --> 09:19.040
And the monarchy was basically based on a very simplistic concept of anatomy.

09:19.040 --> 09:23.400
The monarchs of the old world didn't have a concept of DNA but they did have a concept

09:23.400 --> 09:24.400
of birthright.

09:24.400 --> 09:26.520
They had a concept of blue blood.

09:26.520 --> 09:30.520
They had the idea that the people who would be in political power should be in political

09:30.520 --> 09:37.360
power because of the blood being passed down from grandfather to father to son and so forth.

09:37.360 --> 09:42.160
The founding fathers rejected that idea and they replaced it with a new anatomical concept

09:42.160 --> 09:46.180
and that concept was all men are created equal.

09:46.180 --> 09:50.620
They leveled that playing field and decided the anatomy that mattered was the commonality

09:50.620 --> 09:57.020
of anatomy not the difference in anatomy and that was a really radical thing to do.

09:57.020 --> 10:00.340
Now they were doing it in part because they were part of an enlightenment system where

10:00.340 --> 10:06.360
two things were growing up together and that was democracy growing up but it was also science

10:06.360 --> 10:08.300
growing up at the same time.

10:08.300 --> 10:10.740
It's really clear if you look at the history of the founding fathers a lot of them were

10:10.740 --> 10:15.460
very interested in science and they were interested in a concept of a naturalistic world.

10:15.460 --> 10:20.620
They were moving away from supernatural explanations and they were rejecting things like a supernatural

10:20.620 --> 10:26.900
concept of power where it transmitted because of a very vague concept of birthright.

10:26.900 --> 10:30.980
They were moving towards a naturalistic concept and if you look for example in the Declaration

10:30.980 --> 10:35.060
of Independence they talk about nature and nature's God.

10:35.060 --> 10:37.140
They don't talk about God and God's nature.

10:37.140 --> 10:41.220
They're talking about the power of nature to tell us who we are.

10:41.220 --> 10:47.100
So as part of that they were coming to us with a concept that was about anatomical commonality

10:47.100 --> 10:50.980
and in doing so they were really setting up in a beautiful way the civil rights movement

10:50.980 --> 10:51.980
for the future.

10:51.980 --> 10:55.140
They didn't think of it that way but they did it for us and it was great.

10:55.140 --> 10:57.280
So what happened years afterwards?

10:57.280 --> 11:02.020
What happened was women for example who wanted the right to vote took the founding fathers

11:02.020 --> 11:07.100
concept of anatomical commonality being more important than anatomical difference and said

11:07.100 --> 11:11.820
the fact that we have a uterus and ovaries is not significant enough in terms of a difference

11:11.820 --> 11:15.840
to mean that we shouldn't have the right to vote, the right to full citizenship, the right

11:15.840 --> 11:18.040
to own property, et cetera, et cetera.

11:18.040 --> 11:20.300
And women successfully argued that.

11:20.300 --> 11:24.580
Next came the successful civil rights movement where we found people like Sojourner Truth,

11:24.580 --> 11:26.920
you know, talking about ain't I a woman.

11:26.920 --> 11:32.500
We find men on the marching lines of the civil rights movement saying I am a man.

11:32.500 --> 11:38.220
Again people of color appealing to a commonality of anatomy over a difference of anatomy.

11:38.220 --> 11:39.340
Again successfully.

11:39.340 --> 11:42.740
We see the same thing with the disability rights movement.

11:42.740 --> 11:47.620
The problem is of course that as we begin to look at all that commonality we have to

11:47.620 --> 11:50.800
begin to question why we maintain certain divisions.

11:50.800 --> 11:55.180
Now mind you I want to maintain some divisions anatomically in our culture.

11:55.180 --> 11:58.860
For example I don't want to give a fish the same rights as a human.

11:58.860 --> 12:01.280
I don't want to say we give up entirely on anatomy.

12:01.400 --> 12:04.840
I don't want to say five year olds should be allowed to consent to sex or consent to

12:04.840 --> 12:05.840
marry.

12:05.840 --> 12:10.320
So there are some anatomical divisions that make sense to me and that I think we should

12:10.320 --> 12:11.320
retain.

12:11.320 --> 12:15.400
But the challenge is trying to figure out which ones they are and why do we retain them

12:15.400 --> 12:16.800
and do they have meaning.

12:16.800 --> 12:20.560
So let's go back to those two beings conceived at the beginning of this talk.

12:20.560 --> 12:26.080
We have two beings both conceived in the middle of 1979 on the exact same day.

12:26.080 --> 12:29.840
Let's imagine one of them, Mary, is born three months prematurely.

12:29.840 --> 12:32.320
So she's born on June 1st, 1980.

12:32.320 --> 12:36.560
Henry by contrast is born a term so he's born on March 1st, 1980.

12:36.560 --> 12:42.020
Simply by virtue of the fact that Mary was born prematurely three months she comes into

12:42.020 --> 12:45.580
all sorts of rights three months earlier than Henry does.

12:45.580 --> 12:49.880
The right to consent to sex, the right to vote, the right to drink.

12:49.880 --> 12:54.460
Henry has to wait for all of that not because he's actually any different in age biologically

12:54.460 --> 12:57.480
except in terms of when he was born.

12:57.480 --> 13:00.640
We find other kinds of weirdness in terms of what their rights are.

13:00.640 --> 13:03.840
Henry by virtue of being assumed to be male, although I haven't told you that he's the

13:03.840 --> 13:09.800
XY one, by virtue of being assumed to be male is now liable to be drafted which Mary does

13:09.800 --> 13:11.600
not have to worry about.

13:11.600 --> 13:16.800
Mary meanwhile cannot in all the states have the same right that Henry has in all the states,

13:16.800 --> 13:18.280
namely the right to marry.

13:18.280 --> 13:23.520
Henry can marry in every state a woman but Mary can only marry today in a few states

13:23.520 --> 13:24.840
a woman.

13:24.840 --> 13:30.260
So we have these anatomical categories that persist that are in many ways sort of problematic

13:30.260 --> 13:36.380
and questionable and the question to me becomes what do we do as our science gets to be so

13:36.380 --> 13:42.440
good in looking at anatomy that we reach the point where we have to admit that a democracy

13:42.440 --> 13:47.260
that's been based on anatomy might start falling apart.

13:47.260 --> 13:50.960
I don't want to give up the science but at the same time it kind of feels sometimes like

13:50.960 --> 13:53.360
the science is coming out from under us.

13:53.360 --> 13:55.320
So where do we go?

13:55.320 --> 13:59.400
It seems like what happens in our culture is a sort of pragmatic attitude.

13:59.400 --> 14:03.000
We have to draw the line somewhere so we will draw the line somewhere but a lot of people

14:03.000 --> 14:05.300
get stuck in a very strange position.

14:05.300 --> 14:10.800
So for example Texas has at one point decided that what it means to marry a man is to mean

14:10.800 --> 14:14.620
that you don't have a Y chromosome and what it means to marry a woman means you do have

14:14.620 --> 14:15.620
a Y chromosome.

14:15.620 --> 14:18.920
Now in practice they don't actually test people for their chromosomes but this is also very

14:18.920 --> 14:24.400
bizarre because of the story I told you at the beginning about androgen sensitivity syndrome.

14:24.400 --> 14:28.660
If we look at one of the founding fathers of modern democracy Dr. Martin Luther King

14:28.660 --> 14:32.080
he offers us something of a solution in his eye of a dream speech.

14:32.080 --> 14:36.300
He says we should judge people based not on the color of their skin but on the content

14:36.300 --> 14:40.800
of their character moving beyond anatomy and I want to say yeah that sounds like a really

14:40.800 --> 14:43.860
good idea but in practice how do you do it?

14:43.860 --> 14:47.120
How do you judge people based on the content of character?

14:47.240 --> 14:51.080
I also want to point out that I'm not sure that is how we should distribute rights in

14:51.080 --> 14:55.240
terms of humans because I have to admit there are some golden retrievers that I know that

14:55.240 --> 14:58.880
are probably more deserving of social services than some humans I know.

14:58.880 --> 15:02.320
I want to also say there are probably also even some yellow Labradors that I know that

15:02.320 --> 15:06.720
are more capable of informed intelligent mature decisions about sexual relations than some

15:06.720 --> 15:08.360
40 year olds that I know.

15:08.360 --> 15:12.760
So how do we operationalize the question of content of character?

15:12.760 --> 15:18.120
It turns out to be really difficult and part of me also wonders what if content of character

15:18.120 --> 15:24.520
turns out to be something that's scannable in the future, able to be seen with an fMRI?

15:24.520 --> 15:26.440
Do we really want to go there?

15:26.440 --> 15:28.000
I'm not sure where we go.

15:28.000 --> 15:32.280
What I do know is that it seems really important to think about the idea of the United States

15:32.280 --> 15:35.360
being in the lead of thinking about this issue of democracy.

15:35.360 --> 15:39.360
We've done a really good job struggling with democracy and I think we would do a good job

15:39.360 --> 15:40.360
in the future.

15:40.360 --> 15:44.600
We don't have the situation that Iran has, for example, where a man who's sexually attracted

15:44.600 --> 15:49.080
to other men is liable to be murdered unless he's willing to submit to a sex change, in

15:49.080 --> 15:51.440
which case he's allowed to live.

15:51.440 --> 15:53.040
We don't have that kind of situation.

15:53.040 --> 15:57.920
I'm glad to say we don't have the kind of situation with a surgeon I talked to a few

15:57.920 --> 16:02.080
years ago who had brought over a set of conjoined twins in order to separate them, partly to

16:02.080 --> 16:03.640
make a name for himself.

16:03.640 --> 16:06.600
But when I was on the phone with him asking why he was going to do the surgery, this was

16:06.600 --> 16:10.600
a very high-risk surgery, his answer was that in this other nation these children were

16:10.600 --> 16:14.040
going to be treated very badly and so he had to do this.

16:14.040 --> 16:19.040
My response to him was, well, have you considered political asylum instead, instead of a separation

16:19.040 --> 16:20.040
surgery?

16:20.040 --> 16:24.440
The United States has offered tremendous possibility for allowing people to be the way they are

16:24.440 --> 16:28.420
without having them to have to be changed for the sake of the state.

16:28.420 --> 16:30.120
So I think we have to be in the lead.

16:30.120 --> 16:34.320
Well, just to close, I want to suggest to you that I've been talking a lot about the

16:34.320 --> 16:39.520
fathers and I want to think about the possibilities of what democracy might look like or might

16:39.520 --> 16:42.940
have looked like if we had more involved the mothers.

16:42.940 --> 16:47.240
And I want to say something a little bit radical for a feminist and that is that I think that

16:47.240 --> 16:51.580
there may be different kinds of insights that can come from different kinds of anatomies,

16:51.580 --> 16:54.760
particularly when we have people thinking in groups.

16:54.760 --> 16:57.640
Now for years, because I've been interested in intersex, I've also been interested in

16:57.640 --> 16:59.440
sex difference research.

16:59.440 --> 17:02.720
And one of the things I've been really interested in is looking at the differences between males

17:02.720 --> 17:06.960
and females in terms of the way they think and operate in the world.

17:06.960 --> 17:12.880
And what we know from cross-cultural studies is that females on average, not everyone,

17:12.880 --> 17:18.480
but on average are more inclined to be very attentive to complex social relations and

17:18.480 --> 17:23.100
to taking care of people who are basically vulnerable within the group.

17:23.100 --> 17:27.140
And so if we think about that, we have an interesting situation on our hands.

17:27.140 --> 17:31.180
Years ago when I was in graduate school, one of my graduate advisors who knew I was interested

17:31.180 --> 17:35.780
in feminism and considered myself a feminist, as I still do, asked a really strange question.

17:35.780 --> 17:39.380
He said, tell me what's feminine about feminism.

17:39.380 --> 17:41.740
And I thought, well, that's the dumbest question I've ever heard.

17:41.740 --> 17:44.500
Feminism is all about undoing stereotypes about gender.

17:44.500 --> 17:46.820
So there's nothing feminine about feminism.

17:46.820 --> 17:49.940
But the more I thought about his question, the more I thought there might be something

17:49.940 --> 17:51.700
feminine about feminism.

17:51.700 --> 17:56.940
That is to say, there might be something on average different about female brains from

17:57.140 --> 18:03.420
male brains that makes us more attentive to deeply complex social relationships and more

18:03.420 --> 18:06.060
attentive to taking care of the vulnerable.

18:06.060 --> 18:11.160
So whereas the fathers were extremely attentive to figuring out how to protect individuals

18:11.160 --> 18:16.480
from the state, it's possible that if we injected more mothers into this concept, what we would

18:16.480 --> 18:22.260
have is more of a concept of not just how to protect, but how to care for each other.

18:22.260 --> 18:26.900
And maybe that's where we need to go in the future when we take democracy beyond anatomy

18:26.900 --> 18:31.260
is to think less about the individual body in terms of the identity and think more about

18:31.260 --> 18:36.460
those relationships so that as we the people try to create a more perfect union, we're

18:36.460 --> 18:38.740
thinking about what we do for each other.

18:38.740 --> 18:39.620
Thank you.

